<section aria-labelledby="introduction">
  <h2 id="introduction">Introduction</h2>
  <p>Before you write a single line of code, you need to be comfortable with the machine you will write it on. This first lesson is about the computer itself: what it is, what its main parts do, and how to handle it safely.</p>
  <p>Think for a moment about where you have seen computers being used: at school, in an office, in a cybercafé. People use them to type letters, calculate results, watch videos and visit websites. A smartphone is also a computing device. All of these follow the same basic idea, which you will learn today.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="learn">
  <h2 id="learn">What you will learn</h2>
  <ul class="tick-list">
    <li>Define a computer in simple language.</li>
    <li>Explain at least three things a computer can do.</li>
    <li>Identify the main parts of a desktop computer or laptop.</li>
    <li>Explain the difference between hardware and software.</li>
    <li>Describe what the keyboard, mouse, monitor and system unit do.</li>
    <li>Follow basic rules for handling a computer safely.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="explanation">
  <h2 id="explanation">Explanation</h2>

  <h3>What is a computer?</h3>
  <p><strong>A computer is an electronic device that receives data, processes it according to instructions, and produces information or a result.</strong></p>
  <p>A calculator shows the idea clearly. You enter 5 and 3, ask it to add them, and it displays 8. A computer works on the same basic principle, but it can do many different kinds of task, depending on the programs and instructions it is given.</p>
  <p>Here are the words in that definition, one at a time:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Term</th><th scope="col">Simple meaning</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Electronic device</th><td>A device that uses electricity and electronic parts to work.</td><td>A laptop or a television.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Data</th><td>Raw facts that have not yet been organised into useful information.</td><td>The numbers 12 and 8.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Process</th><td>To work on something according to instructions.</td><td>Adding 12 and 8.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Instructions</th><td>Directions telling a device what to do.</td><td>“Add these two numbers.”</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Information</th><td>Data that has been processed into something meaningful.</td><td>The answer, 20.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Output</th><td>The result a computer gives you.</td><td>The number shown on the screen.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note"><strong>Remember:</strong> a computer does not automatically know what you want. It follows the instructions it receives from software and from the person using it.</p>

  <h3>Input, processing, output and storage</h3>
  <p>Every task a computer does involves some of these four activities:</p>
  <dl class="term-list">
    <div><dt>Input</dt><dd>Anything you give the computer, such as typed text, numbers or instructions. <em>Example: typing students’ marks.</em></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Processing</dt><dd>The work the computer does on the input. <em>Example: calculating the total of the marks.</em></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Output</dt><dd>The result the computer produces. <em>Example: the total displayed on the screen.</em></dd></div>
    <div><dt>Storage</dt><dd>Keeping data or results so you can use them later. <em>Example: saving the results in a file.</em></dd></div>
  </dl>
  <p>A teacher preparing examination results uses all four. The teacher enters five students’ marks (input), a spreadsheet program calculates the average (processing), the average appears on the screen (output), and the teacher saves the file (storage).</p>

  <h3>The main parts of a computer</h3>
  <p>On a desktop computer these parts are usually separate. On a laptop, most of them are built into one machine.</p>
  <dl class="term-list">
    <div><dt>Monitor</dt><dd>The screen that displays text, pictures, videos and other results. It is like a television screen that shows the computer’s work.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Keyboard</dt><dd>An input device with keys for letters, numbers, symbols and commands.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Mouse</dt><dd>A pointing device that moves the pointer on the screen so you can select items, click and open files.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>System unit</dt><dd>The main case of a desktop computer. It holds important parts such as the processor, memory and storage. It is not the same as the monitor.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Touchpad</dt><dd>The flat, touch-sensitive surface on most laptops. Sliding your finger across it moves the pointer, just as a mouse does.</dd></div>
  </dl>

  <h3>Hardware and software</h3>
  <p>You will meet these two words again and again when you learn web development.</p>
  <p><strong>Hardware</strong> means the physical parts of a computer that you can touch: the keyboard, mouse, monitor, system unit, a printer or a USB flash drive. If a computer were a house, the hardware would be the building and the objects inside it.</p>
  <p><strong>Software</strong> means the programs that tell the computer what to do. A <strong>program</strong> is a set of instructions for carrying out a task. Examples are Microsoft Word for writing documents, Google Chrome for opening websites, and Visual Studio Code (VS Code) for writing code, which you will use in this course.</p>
  <p>Hardware and software work together. A keyboard on its own cannot prepare an examination paper; you also need a program that accepts and organises the words you type.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="demonstration">
  <h2 id="demonstration">Demonstration</h2>
  <p>Sit in front of a computer and follow along. You do not need to write any code yet.</p>
  <h3>1. Find the main parts</h3>
  <p>Point to the monitor, the keyboard and the mouse or touchpad. On a desktop computer, find the system unit. Say out loud what each one does.</p>
  <h3>2. Use the mouse or touchpad</h3>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Action</th><th scope="col">What it means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Point</th><td>Move the pointer to an item on the screen.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Click</th><td>Press and release the left mouse button once.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Double-click</th><td>Click twice quickly, often to open an item on the desktop.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Right-click</th><td>Open a menu of extra options in many applications.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Scroll</th><td>Move through content that does not fit on the screen.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Drag</th><td>Hold down the button while moving an item or selecting an area.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Practise each one: click a desktop icon, open an application, scroll through a window and close it with the × button. Computers are set up differently, so a double-click may behave slightly differently on yours.</p>
  <h3>3. Find these keys</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><kbd>Spacebar</kbd> puts a space between words.</li>
    <li><kbd>Enter</kbd> starts a new line in many text applications, or confirms an action.</li>
    <li><kbd>Backspace</kbd> deletes the character to the left of the cursor.</li>
    <li><kbd>Shift</kbd> types a capital letter, or the upper symbol on a key, while you hold it down.</li>
    <li><kbd>Caps Lock</kbd> turns continuous capital letters on or off.</li>
    <li>The <kbd>arrow keys</kbd> move the cursor.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="practice">
  <h2 id="practice">Hands-on practice</h2>
  <div class="practice">
    <p class="practice-title">Exercise 1: Name the parts</p>
    <p>Work with a partner. One of you points to a part of the computer; the other names it and explains what it does. Then swap.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="practice">
    <p class="practice-title">Exercise 2: Type your first sentence</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Open a simple text application such as Notepad. Ask for help if you cannot find it.</li>
      <li>Type your name and one sentence about yourself.</li>
      <li>Explain which part of the computer you used to enter the text, and where the text appeared.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <div class="practice">
    <p class="practice-title">Exercise 3: Hardware or software?</p>
    <p>Sort these into hardware and software, and give a reason for each: keyboard, Google Chrome, monitor, VS Code, mouse, Microsoft Word.</p>
    <details>
      <summary>Show the answers</summary>
      <p>Hardware: keyboard, monitor, mouse (you can touch them). Software: Google Chrome, VS Code, Microsoft Word (they are programs).</p>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="check">
  <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
  <p>A teacher types three marks into a computer: 10, 15 and 20. The computer adds them and displays 45. The teacher saves the result in a document.</p>
  <ol class="check-list">
    <li>What is the input?</li>
    <li>What processing takes place?</li>
    <li>What is the output?</li>
    <li>What does saving the result mean?</li>
    <li>What is the difference between a monitor and a system unit?</li>
  </ol>
  <details>
    <summary>Show the answers</summary>
    <ol>
      <li>The marks 10, 15 and 20.</li>
      <li>Addition.</li>
      <li>45, shown on the screen.</li>
      <li>Keeping the result so it can be used later (storage).</li>
      <li>The monitor is the screen that displays results; the system unit is the case that holds the processor, memory and storage.</li>
    </ol>
  </details>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="mistakes">
  <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Mistake</th><th scope="col">What to do instead</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Calling the monitor “the computer” or “the system unit”.</td><td>The monitor only displays results. The processing happens in the system unit (or inside the laptop).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Thinking the computer decides what to do by itself.</td><td>It follows instructions from software and from you.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Switching off by pulling out the plug.</td><td>Shut down properly from the Start menu so work is not lost.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Eating or drinking beside the computer.</td><td>Keep food and drinks away, and use clean, dry hands.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Working for a long time without saving.</td><td>Save your work regularly.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="summary">
  <h2 id="summary">Lesson summary</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A computer receives data, processes it according to instructions and produces a result.</li>
    <li>Input, processing, output and storage describe how a computer handles information.</li>
    <li>The monitor, keyboard, mouse or touchpad and system unit are the main parts you will use.</li>
    <li>Hardware is what you can touch; software is the programs that tell the hardware what to do.</li>
    <li>Handle the computer carefully and save your work regularly.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="next">
  <h2 id="next">What comes next</h2>
  <p>You now know that a computer stores information. In <a href="<%= lessonUrl(next.slug) %>">Lesson 2</a>, you will learn how that information is organised into files and folders, and you will create the course folder that will hold every webpage you build in this program.</p>
</section>
